== ARCHIVE: ORCA 6.0.1 recomputes the CCSD(T) counterpoise anchor on three systems, with basis identity checked on the AO dimension ==

Why this execution exists. Two things changed since
`20261001T044833Z_orca_crosscheck.out`: the plan was widened from the water dimer to water
(6-31G* and aug-cc-pVDZ), ammonia (6-31G*) and methane (6-31G*) - the same three systems the
PySCF ladder covers, so no row of the anchor is single-program - and the way the two programs
are shown to be solving in the same basis was found to be wrong in the previous file.

The repair, stated plainly because it is the kind of error that passes silently. That file's
header claimed the two programs "report 18 basis functions for a water monomer in 6-31G*, so
the d shells carry the same convention", and its prose said this was "checked rather than
assumed". The field it read, ORCA's `Number of basis functions`, is not a usable identity
criterion: widening the plan showed that for the same water monomer in aug-cc-pVDZ that field
prints 47, while ORCA's own SCF section prints `Basis Dimension Dim .... 41` and its coupled
cluster section `Number of AO's ... 41` - and 41 is PySCF's nao for that basis. The 6-31G*
agreement at 18 was two fields happening to coincide in one basis, not a check. We could not
account for the 47 from the printed output either (25 shells with maximum angular momentum 2:
no Cartesian-versus-spherical bookkeeping reproduces both 47 and 41), so we do not explain it
here; we quote the field that both programs mean the same way by, and note that where the two
ORCA fields do agree (6-31G*: 18, 40, 44) the header field equals the AO dimension anyway.

So the script now reads back, for every one of the 24 runs (dimer plus two monomers, frozen
core and all-electron, four plan rows), ORCA's AO dimension against the PySCF nao for the same
geometry and basis, and prints `same space` or `*** DIFFERENT SPACE ***`. It reports 24/24
same space. The verdict also refuses to say "agree" unless both conditions hold at once -
energy difference under the bar AND basis identity on every part - because a 2e-4 agreement
between two different basis sets would not be agreement at all.

What was measured. Worst |ORCA - PySCF| on the counterpoise interaction energy is 0.0002
kcal/mol against a bar of 0.001, across all eight rows. The row-by-row differences are
-0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0002 (water, 6-31G* then aug-cc-pVDZ), +0.0001, -0.0000
(ammonia) and +0.0001, +0.0000 (methane). Ammonia and methane are new in this execution and
they land at the same place as water, which matters because the water row is the one the
paper's argument is built on: the anchor is no longer a single-system cross-program check.

Repeatability between this execution and the previous one, measured rather than asserted: all
24 absolute energies are reproduced, and the largest single-energy difference between the two
executions is 1.09e-08 Ha, which is 6.8e-06 kcal/mol - three orders of magnitude below the
cross-program difference it sits under. So the 1e-4 kcal/mol scatter seen in the previous
file's two executions shows up in the *interaction energy*, where three independently noisy
energies are combined, not in the individual CCSD(T) evaluations.

Producer: `orca_crosscheck.py` in this directory, run as
`PYTHONPATH=. python3 -u evidence/ccsdt/orca_crosscheck.py` on WSL with ORCA 6.0.1 at
`~/orca6/orca`, `%pal nprocs 4`, `%maxcore 4000`. The PySCF column it compares against is
transcribed into the script from
`20260930T235900Z_stepm_three_systems_631g.out` and
`20260930T224400Z_stepm_ladder_after_refactor.out`, and the comparison is refused if any part
lacks an AO count. Stdout follows verbatim.

